{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe edit-then-re-review loop is common and hazardous as two shell commands (the review must follow the edit; field incidents lost attestations to mis-sequencing), and claim's recovery text (src/rebar/_commands/gates.py:213-215) tells the user to run review-plan then claim by hand. Operator approved both flags. OSS precedent favors embedding prerequisite verification in the consuming verb (cargo publish verifies by default; terraform apply consumes a staleness-checked plan artifact) over new fused commands.\n\n## Approach\n(1) `rebar edit <id> ... --review`: in edit_cli (src/rebar/_commands/composer.py:506-573), accept `--review` as a non-field flag; after edit_core commits the EDIT event, invoke rebar.llm.review_plan(ticket_id, sign=True) in the foreground. review_plan returns a verdict dict, not an exit code: the flag maps the dict to the process exit status via _cli._llm_commands._disposition_exit_code(result, indeterminate_code=2) — the same mapping the review-plan CLI applies (0 PASS / 1 BLOCK / 2 INDETERMINATE / 11 retryable per ADR 0040). (2) `rebar claim <id> --review`: at the precheck call (src/rebar/_commands/claim.py:133), on a plan-review CommandError run review_plan(sign=True), then re-run plan_review_precheck and proceed on success; the flag is NOT propagated through the parent-first cascade (a surprise multi-minute parent review is a bad default). Both flags are CLI-only (the MCP surface keeps its REBAR_MCP_ALLOW_LLM fence) and their help text states they are not atomic against concurrent store reconvergence, pointing at `review-plan --status` as the cheap currency probe. Lock discipline: neither flag holds the store flock across the review — the edit/claim appends its own event under the normal short-lived lock, releases it, and only then invokes review_plan, which itself takes the lock only for the brief under-lock generation re-check at signing (src/rebar/llm/plan_review/generation.py sign path); the multi-minute LLM passes run with no lock held. Alternative rejected: new fused verbs (edit-review/claim-review) — no OSS precedent, larger surface.\n\n## Scope\nsrc/rebar/_commands/composer.py, src/rebar/_commands/claim.py, src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py (help), tests/unit/test_review_flags.py (new), docs/cli-reference.md.\n\n## Testing\nUnit with a stubbed review_plan returning verdict dicts: edit --review runs review after the EDIT commit and maps PASS/BLOCK/INDETERMINATE dicts to exits 0/1/2 via _disposition_exit_code; a BLOCK leaves the edit committed and exits 1; claim --review reviews then claims on PASS, fails closed with the standard message when the post-review recheck still fails; the parent cascade never receives the flag.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] edit --review runs the plan review strictly after the EDIT event is committed and exits with _disposition_exit_code(result, indeterminate_code=2) applied to the returned verdict dict (checked: unit test with stubbed review_plan recording call order and asserting exit codes for PASS/BLOCK/INDETERMINATE stubs).\n- [ ] claim --review runs review-plan only when the precheck fails, then re-checks and claims on PASS (checked: unit test).\n- [ ] The parent-first cascade does not inherit --review (checked: unit test claiming a child with an open parent).\n- [ ] Both help texts document non-atomicity and reference review-plan --status (checked: help-output test).\n- [ ] The store lock is not held while the review runs: a stubbed review_plan that acquires the store flock itself succeeds when invoked via either flag (checked: unit test whose review stub takes the lock)."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785388162382210001,"uuid":"84436b44-55a1-44cb-abea-e3c2a8bd50b5"}